# Security headers.
#
# Read natively by Cloudflare Pages and Netlify. Vite copies this file to the
# build output verbatim. On a host that ignores it (GitHub Pages, S3) set the
# same headers there instead — the site works without them, it is just less
# defended.
#
# The Content-Security-Policy is the important line. Every card on the three
# hub pages is built from data fetched out of somebody else's system, and while
# each value is now encoded on output, a CSP is the layer that holds when a
# single interpolation is missed.
#
# script-src is 'self' with no 'unsafe-inline'. That matters: 'unsafe-inline'
# is exactly what permits an injected `onerror=` handler, so a policy carrying
# it would not have blocked the injection this replaced. Every inline script
# was moved into a file (assets/js/theme-init.js, assets/js/hub.js) to make
# this possible.
#
# style-src keeps 'unsafe-inline' — the markup and the hub scripts both set
# style attributes, and CSS injection is a far weaker vector than script.
#
# form-action 'none' is correct even with a contact form: the form never
# submits, it posts through fetch(), which connect-src governs.
#
# connect-src is an ALLOWLIST, so it has to be kept in step with the proxy pool
# in assets/js/news.js. It had drifted: it still named allorigins, codetabs and
# thingproxy (all removed from the code) and named neither corsfix nor
# cors.eu.org (both added). Deployed like that, the browser would have blocked
# the only proxy that can reach BleepingComputer, CrowdStrike, DataBreaches.net
# and the 1.5MB CISA KEV catalogue — reproducing the original "news is slow and
# half-empty" bug, but only in production, where it is hardest to spot.
#
# The feed hosts themselves (cisa.gov, bleepingcomputer.com, ...) are NOT listed
# and must not be: they are fetched server-side by the proxy, so the browser
# only ever connects to the proxy origin.

/*
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self'; worker-src 'self'; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; img-src 'self' data: https:; font-src 'self'; connect-src 'self' https://api.web3forms.com https://api.rss2json.com https://api.github.com https://services.nvd.nist.gov https://en.wikipedia.org https://api.duckduckgo.com https://api.mathjs.org https://corsproxy.io https://proxy.corsfix.com https://cors.eu.org; frame-src https://drive.google.com; form-action 'none'; base-uri 'none'; object-src 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'; upgrade-insecure-requests
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Permissions-Policy: geolocation=(), microphone=(), camera=(), payment=(), usb=(), magnetometer=(), accelerometer=()
  Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains

# The service worker must never be cached by the HTTP layer, or a change to
# the caching rules could not reach a browser that already has the old one.
/sw.js
  Cache-Control: no-cache
  Service-Worker-Allowed: /

# Hashed filenames are immutable, so they can be cached for a year.
/assets/*.js
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

/assets/*.css
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# The hub scripts are not hashed and are edited in place, so they must revalidate.
/assets/js/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

/assets/hub.css
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

/assets/data/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600
